<BaseLayout title="Wiring" description="Passenger fuse box, push-to-start, Holley cranking power, reverse lights, and brake switch wiring for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Wiring</h1>
  <p>
    Wiring is the most year-specific part of an LS Gen Coupe swap. Colors and pinouts change between BK1/BK2 and 2.0T/3.8 models,
    so verify with a multimeter and your factory service manuals before cutting or splicing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shop manuals &amp; wiring diagrams:</strong>
    <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1p-_n4A5JZTp92iz3rK1sQcm1jPLSvz5W?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Drive folder (all years/models)</a>
  </p>

  <nav class="toc">
    <a href="#fuse-box">Passenger fuse box</a>
    <a href="#push-to-start">Push to start</a>
    <a href="#holley-power">Holley cranking power</a>
    <a href="#auto-reverse">Reverse lights (auto)</a>
    <a href="#manual-reverse">Reverse lights (manual)</a>
    <a href="#tcc">Torque converter unlock</a>
  </nav>
  <p>
    Related wiring lives on its own pages: <a href="/guides/fuel-system#fuel-pump-relay">fuel pump relay</a>,
    <a href="/guides/cooling#fans">radiator fans &amp; relays</a>, <a href="/guides/alternator">alternator</a>,
    <a href="/guides/ac">A/C</a>, <a href="/guides/gas-pedal">gas pedal</a>, and <a href="/guides/ecu#speedo">VSS / speedo</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fuse-box">Passenger-Side Fuse Box</h2>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Note:</strong> If you want to keep the factory A/C function, depending on how you wire the new engine ECU, keep the passenger-side fuse box and the stock ECU. Otherwise check the <a href="/guides/ac">A/C page</a> for details.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The main wires you will need from here:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Large pink</strong> — wire this directly to your starter.</li>
    <li><strong>Red/white</strong> — the OEM fuel pump power wire. Use it as a trigger wire: attach it to the Holley green FP wire if you already have a dedicated FP relay. You can also find this wire near the driver's kick panel if you don't want it in the engine bay.</li>
    <li><strong>Large red</strong> — several switched-on 12V feeds. Leave a few open for easy access down the road (relays, accessories), or just tap a fuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Twisted red/blue pair</strong> — CAN bus. You most likely won't need these.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="passenger-fuse-box-labeled.jpg" alt="Passenger side fuse box wires labeled 12V, FP, A/C, CAN and Starter" caption="Wires pulled from the passenger-side box: 12V, FP, A/C, CAN, and starter." />
    <Figure src="passenger-fuse-box-pink-starter.jpg" alt="Hand holding pink starter wire and harness pulled from passenger fuse box" caption="Harness opened up — the large pink starter wire." />
  </div>
  <p>
    Some people open up the whole harness and pull out the extra wires. Personally I just capped the extras and wrapped them back up —
    it looks just as clean, but it's up to you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="push-to-start">Push to Start</h2>
  <p>
    The pink starter wire provides 12V when you press in the clutch pedal and the Push to Start button. It attaches directly to the small stud on the starter.
  </p>
  <p>
    On some other starters the pink wire won't work by itself — you first have to ground the starter relay, then use the pink wire to the starter.
  </p>
  <Figure src="push-to-start-wiring.jpg" alt="Pink push-to-start wire routed in engine bay toward the starter" caption="Pink push-to-start wire routed toward the starter." />

  <h2 id="holley-power">Holley 12V Power While Cranking</h2>
  <p>
    A Holley Terminator X setup needs 12V power while cranking. Use the driver's-side fuse box, 2nd row, third down, labeled <em>ECU power</em> —
    the Holley ECU power wire goes here.
  </p>
  <Figure src="ecu-power-fuse-tap.jpg" alt="Fuse tap installed in driver side fuse box ECU power slot" caption="Fuse tap in the driver's-side box ECU power slot." />

  <h2 id="auto-reverse">Automatic Reverse Lights</h2>
  <blockquote>
    "Wired up backup lights for T56. Used a short reverse sensor from Sikky and a pigtail. Used it as the ground switch for the backup lights relay on the
    driver's-side fuse box, so one wire ground, one up to the switch side of the relay. There should be current going to the relay. I spliced the always-on 12V
    for the relay to also act as the positive side of the switch. Relay numbering perspective is top-down, as if you took the relay out and are looking at the female connectors."
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Slot 1</strong> spliced with <strong>slot 3</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Slot 5</strong> goes to the reverse switch</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="reverse-light-harness-location.jpg" alt="Reverse light relay location in driver side fuse box with arrow" caption="Reverse light relay location." />
    <Figure src="reverse-sensor-harness.jpg" alt="Braided reverse sensor harness made for T56" caption="Reverse sensor harness." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="manual-reverse">Manual Reverse Lights</h2>
  <div class="note">
    <p>Manuals should note the reverse switch connector on the OEM transmission if you want to reuse the passenger-side fuse box relays or wires. (No pictures or pinouts for these yet.)</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    If you have a T56-variant transmission and no passenger-side relay box, simply run a long pigtail so the reverse lights work when you shift into reverse.
    Run a fuse power tap from the driver's-side fuse panel (seat heater shown below) to the green side of the reverse switch on the passenger side of the transmission.
    Then take the black switch lead and connect it to the corner white wire under the driver's kick panel.
  </p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="reverse-fuse-box-diagram.jpg" alt="Driver side fuse panel with fuse label diagram" caption="Driver's-side fuse panel — tap the seat heater fuse for power." />
    <Figure src="reverse-fuse-box-pin12.jpg" alt="Diagram of driver side cabin fuse box rear view with wire 12 marked" caption="Rear view of the in-cab fuse box: tap wire #12 (may be grey or red/black)." />
    <Figure src="t56-reverse-sensor-trans.jpg" alt="T56 transmission with reverse switch pigtail attached" caption="Pigtail made for the T56 reverse switch." />
    <Figure src="muzzys-t56-reverse-pigtail.jpg" alt="Muzzys T56 reverse lamp pigtail product listing" caption="Muzzy's T56 pigtail set (back-up lamp, lockout, VSS)." />
  </div>
  <p><strong>2016 BK2</strong> might be this wire — the gray wire at the tail light is reverse; provide it with power and it lights up:</p>
  <Figure src="reverse-tail-light-gray-wire.jpg" alt="Message screenshot showing gray reverse wire at BK2 tail light" caption="BK2: gray wire at the tail light is reverse (unconfirmed on all years)." />

  <h2 id="tcc">Automatic Torque Converter Unlock Signal</h2>
  <p>Terminator X brake light switch hookup for BK2: run the <strong>grey</strong> wire from the Term X transmission harness to the <strong>red/black</strong> wire from the brake switch.</p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="brake-switch-connector.jpg" alt="Brake switch connector with red/black wire" caption="Brake switch connector." />
    <Figure src="stop-lamp-switch-diagram.jpg" alt="Stop lamp switch wiring diagram" caption="Stop lamp switch diagram." />
  </div>

